WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.880
 So Yogi Bhai, thanks for making the time to have this conversation with us today.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:13.540
 Thank you.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.300
 You've had a bit of a fun journey.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.980
 We've known each other since maybe we were two or three years old.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.020
 But you've had a very interesting life, teaching at Columbia, lecturing at universities around

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:24.480
 the world.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.300
 And all along the way, I think you've also, from whatever I've seen, had a very unique

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.100
 experience of living a life with Pramukh Swayamaj, which you've also put into a book in Love at

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:36.320
 Ease.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.860
 But today, I really was eager to talk to you for something you've done more recently.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.020
 So 2024, you got your PhD thesis.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:45.880
 Congratulations.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.320
 It took a lifetime.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.860
 I want to hear about that.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.820
 So we did that, and you did that, and it was about the Paramansas and their bhakti poetry,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.100
 if I understand that, right?

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.140
 And that's something that I was very curious to know about, because the only stuff that I

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.980
 know about bhajans and kirtans is what I've heard in Sabha, and I haven't really been able

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.600
 to go beyond that in my education.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.840
 So I was really eager to have you here, so I'm happy you had the time today.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.100
 But I do want to start off right away with that thesis, in terms of, you're a journalism

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.480
 person, you're speaking at CBS New York, you're doing all of that.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.760
 How does that pivot into bhakti poetry and doing this PhD?

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.780
 Probably 20 years ago, 18, 19 years ago.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.280
 Really a lifetime.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.840
 Really, a lifetime, at least a generation.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.800
 When I was on air, I had a conversation with my guru, His Holiness Pramukh Sai Maharaj.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:50.260
 And he didn't specifically tell me to go study in the academy.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.600
 What he said to me was quite fascinating.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:02.540
 When you think of the sort of broad-minded vision that gurus, in general, have for their

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.060
 disciples.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.480
 Pramukh Sai Maharaj said to me, that Yogi, everyone does something for themselves, their

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.540
 families, their own careers, things that help their pratishta, is the word he used.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:15.780
 Their standing.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.140
 Fame, standing, position.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.620
 But what are we doing to give back to society?

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.600
 That is the question I want you to ask yourself.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.020
 And so I thought, in my mind, maybe he's asking me to be a monk.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.780
 Maybe he's asking me to give back by doing some humanitarian service of sorts.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.800
 And I think what he pointed at was education.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.040
 He said, what do you know about Hinduism?

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.340
 What do you know about the Swaminar and Sampradai?

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.920
 And how can then, after educating yourself, how can you share that with our community, the

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.440
 broader Hindu community, and those who are curious about engaging with Hinduism and the

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.280
 Swaminar and Sampradai?

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.940
 And as you know, there are millions who are.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.520
 If you just think of all of the Swaminar and Mandirs around the world, specifically BAPS

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:08.260
 Mandirs, and you think of how many visitors we have, that means those people are curious to

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.200
 know more about Hinduism, and more specifically the Swaminar and Sampradai.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:21.440
 And what are we doing to share important aspects and facets of our past, of our present, of our music

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.580
 and literature, and some of our guiding principles with them, is what I got from my conversation

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.580
 with Pramuk Saimara.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.780
 This is like 2004, 5, 2, 3?

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:32.620
 Right about that time.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.440
 Right about that time.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.840
 I was on air 4, 5, 6 around that time.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.480
 So when I say on air, I was at CBS.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.680
 And so that was the time when I made the shift.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:51.720
 I went to Columbia University, sat in on a course with my academic guruji, John Stratton

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.400
 Hawley, who's more popularly known as Jack Hawley.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.000
 And Jack Hawley Sahib, I sat in on a course.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.820
 I remember it was right up my alley.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.280
 It was called North Indian Bhakti texts.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.500
 That's something they teach at Columbia?

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:07.660
 At Columbia, yeah.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.860
 See, these are things we don't know, that this sort of stuff is in the zeitgeist or at least

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.500
 in academia already, yeah?

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.180
 Yeah, and quite popular.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.200
 Quite popular, that course.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:27.920
 We read, and in my case sang, Surdas Ji's pads, Kabir Ji's pads, Ravidas Ji's pads, Mirabai's

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.900
 pads, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.460
 And I think that's when it clicked for me.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.280
 And I said, this is what I wanted to do.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.760
 Then came up the conversation about what it is that I would study, right?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:48.740
 Because you have to start off with a broad understanding of world religions, Hinduism,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:58.300
 and then hone in on this one particular topic that you really want to get your Ph.D.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:03.200
 So we all speak Gujarati, so you'll understand this joke that a bunch of my friends, especially

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.020
 here in India, have.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:04.900
 Your Ph.D.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.640
 So what is that one topic that you really want to dive into?

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.040
 And I think that one course with Jack Hawley Sahib really made me start thinking about the

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.520
 Swaminar and Sampradai and the sort of treasure, the trove of Bhakti pads that we have.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.680
 Before we get too far at the pads, I want to take you back a second.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.360
 So we can kind of maybe wrap up the diversity part.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:27.680
 Okay.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.400
 Not that there's such a thing as wrapping it up.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:35.000
 But like, so we had started this whole section about diversity by talking about how these,

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.120
 even the four poets that you had chosen were diverse.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:45.280
 Brahman and Swami is the poet's poet, very structured and traditional in a Charan tradition.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.280
 And you said foremost poets and educators and then performers in a second kind of level.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:57.740
 And whereas you said Preman and Swami is more of the musician and, you know, his voice and

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.680
 other instruments are his instruments, poetry to him was the second thing.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.060
 And that's why sometimes maybe he would break meter and do those things.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.760
 And very diverse as a musician.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.820
 He was singing ragas, different languages, but also different genres.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:15.960
 So if you think of ragas, dhrupad, khayal, we won't get into all of this, but different

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.340
 genres of classical all the way down to folk.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.540
 And if folkma, the one thing that we should mention are his garbis, garbis, which are usually

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:33.860
 pads that can be sung and then repeated by a larger audience.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:39.680
 And those garbis became so popular in the sampradai and outside of the sampradai.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:47.400
 In fact, Dalpat Ramji, who was, you know, celebrated by many in Gujarat as the sort of father of modern

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.560
 literature, Gujarati modern literature, he had said that once he heard Preman and Swami

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.460
 singa garbis and it was so effective.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.220
 He was about Maharaj's dhamagaman.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.180
 So he's going, reverting to Akshar dham.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.140
 And he goes, and so many years, I think 11 or 12 years after Maharaj dhamagya,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:15.900
 and that environment charged that many people started crying, so there was this power in

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.880
 his music, this power in the words, but also in the performance of those words.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.600
 So that's how diverse Preman is.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.880
 He, yeah, I mean, we could go on for Preman and Swami for a while, because I love him as

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:26.620
 a poet as well.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.320
 But then you said Muktanan Swami and Nishkanan Swami.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:31.220
 These were the four.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.680
 So these two you've gone through, how do you categorize Muktanan Swami and Nishkanan Swami?

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:42.900
 So Muktanan Swami was, from what we understand, and I have a lot more work left to do on Muktanan

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.580
 Swami, I admit that first and foremost, but Muktanan Swami was raised among Shaivas.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:57.840
 In fact, one of my colleagues who has written about this, Professor Arun Brahmat, often talks

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:05.040
 about the sort of influence of Kashmiri Shaivism on Narsi Mehta, and then later, if I understand

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.900
 correctly, if I recall correctly, on Muktanan Swami as well.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:14.400
 So this lasting effect of being raised in a Shaiva background, which is very different from

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.160
 a Vaishnav background, and I really just don't have the time to get into that.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.580
 But even Hinduism was so diverse at the time, right?

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:28.500
 If you think of Vaishnavism and the sort of great overall bhakti of Vishnu and the avatars

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.660
 of Vishnuji, Vishnu Bhagavan and Krishna Bhagavan, etc., on that one side.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.320
 Shaivism and...

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.780
 And then Shaktism.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.780
 And then Shaktism and the Mataji and the goddess worship on that side.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:45.920
 And how diverse all three of them were in terms of language, theology, cultural repertoire,

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.560
 practice, dietary habits.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.200
 We can keep going on and on and on.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:57.100
 And yet amidst all of that, in that diversity that Muktanan Swami had from his Shaiva and Shakti

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.700
 background, some influence from his interactions with Jains as well, etc.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:12.200
 And all of that, Muktanan Swami was able to come to Gujarat, become Ramanan Swami, who was Bhagavan

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:14.640
 Swami Narayan's guru, as we know, his Shishya.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:23.240
 And then being the senior disciple of Ramanan Swami, then accepting Bhagavan Swami Narayan as his...

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:27.560
 Not just his guru, because Ramanan Swami was his guru, but his Ishtadev, right?

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.440
 This is another thing we forget.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:36.240
 Most of these people were so accomplished, especially when you think of Muktanan Swami and

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.580
 Brahmanan Swami, before ever having met Bhagavan Swami Narayan.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:46.960
 We're so accomplished, and then to accept Bhagavan Swami Narayan as their Ishtadev, and we'll

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:54.680
 get into what that means, like not just a spiritual master, but as the supreme being, supreme divine

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.560
 being, Purnapushottam Narayan, Parabrahman.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:03.580
 And I think to be able to do that, and while doing that, Muktanan Swami was also writing in

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:05.600
 five or six different languages, his poetry.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.500
 Muktanan Swami was also writing in various genres.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.580
 Muktanan Swami was a great dancer.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:19.480
 So Muktanan Swami, there's a very, I forget exactly which vat text, was a Prasadhan Swami's

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:27.140
 vat, where Muktanan Swami, he was able to draw a peacock.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:35.000
 So you just imagine the immense amounts of, and diverse amounts of talent that these Paramahans

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:41.700
 were, and so that was Muktanan Swami with the remnants of these different other religious

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.040
 and sectarian communities.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.840
 And then finally, Nishkuralan Swami.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:53.680
 Also multi-talented, carpenter, and meets Gunatana Swami at a young age, they're doing

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.980
 ghoshti from their original sampradai, then becomes a sadhu through a journey.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:03.580
 What's his kind of musical pedigree, as you put them in this like four, set of four?

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:08.620
 I would describe Nishkuralan Swami, and it's so hard to say in black and white.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:09.880
 Yeah, we don't mention all of them.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:10.360
 Brahmanswami abad che.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:10.940
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:11.760
 No, because you can't.

00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:12.280
 You can't.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:12.480
 Right?

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:13.820
 And we'll get into that when we talk.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:16.820
 Like you're talking about Muktanan Swami's dancing, and you've got Brahmanswami who's

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.900
 like speaking to the royal courts to build these mandirs and doing all these things.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:30.420
 So we're talking about Nishkuralan Swami, the one thing that hits me about Nishkuralan

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:35.040
 Swami was that he was much older than Premanan Swami.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:47.640
 He had seen life before he became a renunciant, he had seen life, and he says it best in a

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:53.300
 pad that's attributed to him, in which he talks about gara gara, at that time gara, like

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.480
 carts, bara dhata, right?

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.260
 Because go dhan at that time.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:04.900
 If you had cows and bulls, you had dhan, or you had wealth at the time, in that sort

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:08.340
 of agrarian society that we, the mercantile society that we lived in.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.720
 So he said, eh ha tu maari paase.

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:14.060
 I had a lot of land on which there was farming happening.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:19.560
 So Nishkuralan Swami himself keha chai, maari paase atlo, atlo, atlo ha tu, eh mukhi

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.220
 ne pashi mein Bhagavan Swami narna hathe dikshali dihi.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:27.920
 And so he was a man who, he was a worldly man, if you see what I'm saying.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:29.220
 He was married.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:30.020
 He had kids.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:31.960
 He left all of that aside.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:33.960
 We'll get to how he did, leaves that aside.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:40.540
 And then after taking niksha, he becomes a tyagi's tyagi.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.020
 You know how we said Brahman and Swami was a poet's poet?

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:43.580
 Poet's poet, yeah.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:44.640
 A tyagi's tyagi.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:45.840
 What do I mean by that?

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:48.420
 Again, we go back to his buds.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:57.500
 Nishkuralan Swami's memory is closely tied to the village, dusty, sleepy village of

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:58.220
 Dolera today.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:04.740
 I think there's this big push right now to make Dolera into this smart city and the current

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.760
 dispensation government is working on that, has been working on it for a while.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:13.440
 But for as long as one can remember it over the last two, three, four hundred...

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.900
 Yeah, even when we've gone like, let's say a decade ago to Dolera or five years ago.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.180
 And there's a beautiful mandir there now.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:25.620
 The current administration is working on this master plan and I truly encourage everyone

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:26.820
 to go see Dolera.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:31.040
 That's when you truly realize Nishkuralan Swami's maima for several reasons.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.280
 You look at the town and then you see the stark difference that the mandir is and has been.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:37.880
 The gate that he's carved with his own hands.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:39.680
 The kamaan.

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:40.080
 Yeah.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.500
 The kamaan is the archway.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:44.160
 And now they've, I think, it's gold-plated.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.440
 So that particular kamaan stands out.

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:50.680
 And so you know that Nishkuralan Swami is not prasad in the kamaan.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:55.180
 So I think those are the kinds of things that I would encourage people to go see.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:14:00.540
 If you want to see that, a quick aside, if you want to see something like that material

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.740
 about Brahman and Swami.

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:02.220
 Muri.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.620
 Muri and Buj, I would recommend it.

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:07.020
 Two places where his disciple Parampara and Junagar.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:13.040
 A lot of Junagar was lost in the, there was a fire in the Tahaveli.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:16.700
 So a lot of his texts and his instruments were lost in that.

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.620
 But Muri and Buj are great places to go visit.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:27.320
 So my point is that Nishkuralan Swami went from being a worldly man to a Tiaghi.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:28.360
 Extreme renunciate.

00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:33.460
 And then I think the one thing that we, all of us, especially those of us who are interested

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:39.660
 in history, have to salute him for, was that he was a true historian, a true record.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:40.520
 In the vernacular.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:41.720
 In the vernacular.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:44.700
 So it's like accessible to the, to every man, right?

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.780
 To be able to read that history themselves.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:52.460
 I don't, the thing about Nishkuralan Swami that I want to kind of hear from you is like,

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:54.360
 did he write before?

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:57.120
 Like, is there poetry before he becomes a Sadhu?

00:14:58.500 --> 00:14:59.640
 It's a good question.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:05.960
 Nothing that, that can be, that we can be traced back in to sort of say, I record Nishkuralan Swami.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.720
 There's no attributed, like, book or this or that to him from before.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:15.340
 And yet, as soon as he becomes a Tiaghi and is joining all this, amongst all of his renunciation,

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:22.500
 this art, this vibrancy of art lives both in his, through his craftsmanship and also through

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:22.940
 this poetry.

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:24.300
 I think that's also interesting.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:29.880
 Usually when people think of, you know, the Tiaghi-Stiaghi, it's somebody sitting in a,

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:35.440
 in a cave or sitting in a mountain or sitting in, you know, the desert just in, in stark

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:36.000
 meditation.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.040
 But here's a person who has extreme simplicity.

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:44.340
 And sometimes I want to move to this topic a little bit later about, you know, the categories

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.200
 we put people in.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:50.320
 But it's like, if you put them in the category of Tiaghi and the Tiaghi-Stiaghi, then you're

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.840
 thinking as famously, you know, dhudhna apiveh, he was like, you know, he was taking extreme

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:56.920
 vows of simplicity.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.280
 Let me tell you what some of those vows are.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:02.600
 And yet vibrancy of life in terms of art is just as rich.

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:03.020
 Exactly.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:09.340
 So this balance between renunciation and the aesthetic, appreciating that.

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:14.520
 And in fact, one of the Vachana Amraths that Bhagavan Swaminarayan, the number is escaping

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:15.020
 my memory.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:22.680
 Actually, no, one of the references to Nishkuran Swamy is not just for his Tiaghi and Vairagya.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:27.400
 But Gunatiyan Swamy said that Nishkuranan Swamy, which is the name of Bhagavan Swaminarayan

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:30.460
 Maathe, which is the name of God, the name of God, the name of God, the name of God.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:35.540
 So this balance of being able to let go, but also to love.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.720
 And I think that is the beauty of bhakti, right?

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.800
 But Nishkuranan Swamy, if I can give you some examples.

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:51.000
 We want to make you the mahanth of Dholera.

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:51.580
 He ran away.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:51.960
 Yeah.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:53.700
 He's like, I'm gone.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:54.740
 Right.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:17:00.560
 And even when that conversation was had for whether to give that to him in Gharada or whatnot as

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:06.440
 well, and then he says in one of his pads that I want future sadhus and my disciples to know

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:08.100
 that what is going on in Gharada?

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:12.540
 And he describes what kind of a gaam Dholera is.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:15.940
 If you don't mind, I just want to quickly pull it up.

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:22.060
 And in it, he goes, you know, don't for a second think that I did this because I want to become

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:23.060
 Power.

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:23.620
 Yeah.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:26.620
 And I want to become some big place because

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:36.260
 You know, Dholera really is that dusty, sleepy town we were talking about, at least.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:37.260
 Water is rare.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:38.940
 Water is scarce there, right?

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:44.260
 There's no large amounts of agricultural produce per se.

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:48.140
 Maybe one or two paak that you're growing there, but you're not getting like four types

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:51.380
 of vegetables in your home garden that's being grown on a regular basis.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:52.020
 Certainly not.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:57.400
 So I feel like, so if we look at this first section of what we've talked about, what I'm

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:06.520
 taking away from that first section is that the times were such that a variety of people

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:07.580
 were coming to Gujarat.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:14.060
 In that kind of ecosystem, Bhagavan Swam Nairn establishes the fellowship and we kind of

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:15.960
 appreciate what established means.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.360
 It's not just coming and planting a flag, but it's saying, you know, building that sense

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:24.760
 of community by instructing people to write books and do things in the vernacular as well

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:26.300
 as in Sanskrit and so many other languages.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.920
 And along with that, beliefs, practices, festivals, right?

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:32.120
 Gajda Pratham 3, where Maharaj talks about

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.740
 To bring people together.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:35.300
 Right.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:39.180
 So he's actively kind of creating this community.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:43.440
 And in his own time, he's allowing a lot of diversity within that community.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:51.740
 So, and the diversity, to be very clear about that is that the philosophical principles are

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.200
 uniform, right?

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.680
 Everybody believes in the same Tattva Gnan, let's say, that Bhagavan Swam Nairn is putting

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:56.980
 out.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:02.440
 Everyone is the Ishtadev, Bhavana, and those things are all there.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:08.120
 But around that, because each of those people come from different backgrounds, not just geographically,

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:12.420
 but even from different faith traditions, like you said, you've got Shave tradition people

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.320
 like Magni Ram and others who are coming in.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:16.740
 You've got Shakta tradition people, you've got Rational tradition people.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.760
 And they're all kind of coalescing around Bhagavan Swam Nairn and seeing him as the Ishtadev

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:22.700
 in that own lifetime.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.600
 Bishop Haber famously mentioned this in his diary, that Bhagavan Swam Nairn travels.

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.720
 And in that travel, there's a diversity of people around him.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:33.540
 Because he probably saw Mia Ji and Ratan Ji and all these people from different backgrounds

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:34.800
 all being there together.

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:40.960
 So Maharaj has, in a very active way, created a diverse community.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:46.240
 And I think that is a lesson I take strongly because I think it identifies this idea that

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:53.840
 there can be people who share values, that share philosophy, that share praxis, and still

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:55.200
 have quite a bit of diversity.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:20:01.260
 We can't, sometimes in our own heads maybe, we start imagining that everybody in satsang

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:01.780
 is the same.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.660
 Or everybody's likes and dislikes and everybody's behavior, because we're trying to go to the

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.100
 same ideal, should be uniform.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:11.320
 But that's not true.

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:15.620
 There's a lot of diversity that is welcomed by Maharaj himself in his time and is welcomed

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:20.240
 today as well and exists today if we're just open enough to receive it, interact with it,

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:22.120
 and take it as part of it.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:26.200
 Thank you.

